+
+## Checking stack mode on real GitHub
+
+The offline suites test stack mode against a local bare `origin` and a stubbed `gh`.
+`tools/live/stack_mode_live.py` (standard library only, not shipped to rendered
+instances) checks the same workflow on GitHub: a disposable private repository, real
+`git`, real `gh`, PRs merged on GitHub. Each run goes through the driver as
+`pdca flow <ids>` does, with the model leaves replaced by fixed patches, so it makes no
+model calls. Run it after a change to stack mode, or when GitHub's behaviour may have
+changed:
+
+```bash
+W=$(mktemp -d)
+python3 tools/live/stack_mode_live.py setup    --workdir "$W"   # creates pdca-live-stack-<UTC time>
+python3 tools/live/stack_mode_live.py run      --workdir "$W"   # writes observations.json
+python3 tools/live/stack_mode_live.py judge    --workdir "$W"   # writes report.md + report.json
+python3 tools/live/stack_mode_live.py teardown --workdir "$W"   # deletes the repository (--keep skips)
+```
+
+It needs an authenticated `gh` whose token has the `repo` and `delete_repo` scopes
+(`gh auth refresh -s delete_repo`). A run creates and deletes one private repository,
+opens seven PRs and takes a few minutes, mostly waiting for GitHub to recompute PR
+diffs (`run --settle SECONDS`, default 15). `setup` refuses to reuse an existing
+repository without `--reuse`, `setup` and `run` refuse a work directory that was already
+used, and `teardown` deletes only a repository whose name starts with `pdca-live-stack-`.
+
+`setup` records the repository in the work directory as soon as GitHub has created it. If
+a later step fails (a refused push, say), `setup` exits 1 and names the repository, `run`
+refuses that work directory, and `teardown` still deletes the repository. If
+`gh repo create` itself fails, `setup` records nothing and prints the `gh repo delete`
+command to run in case GitHub made the repository anyway.
+
+`judge` reads only `observations.json` and needs no network, so it can be re-run on a
+saved file. It exits 0 only if every check passes. The report's result is PASS, FAIL or
+INCOMPLETE. INCOMPLETE means `run` could not drive the scenario to its end (a run of the
+flow that left a bundle unfinished or opened no PR for it, a merge or branch delete GitHub
+refused): `run` exits 1 and says why. That is a problem with the run, not a stack-mode
+finding; a check that depends on a step that never happened FAILs with `not observed`.
+The checks have stable ids:
+
+- `E0`: all seven PRs were opened, each with the real base as its base, never the
+  integration line.
+- `E1`: a PR on two merged wave-0 branches shows only its own files after "Update branch".
+- `E1b`: in a plain chain, the dependent PR shows only its own files once its prerequisite
+  merges, with no "Update branch".
+- `E2`: a PR merged with a merge commit and its branch deleted still reports `state`
+  `MERGED`, the head the run pushed as `headRefOid`, and a `mergeCommit` on the base.
+- `E3`: a later run that names the merged ids again carries their deleted branches in
+  through the base and finishes: the fold says each branch is gone and merged and goes
+  on, the run exits 0 with no holds, and the new dependent is built on a line holding the
+  merged work.
+- `E4`: after a squash merge, `mergeCommit` is on the base and `headRefOid` is not.
+- `E5`: merging the stack bottom-up puts every change on the base.
+
+`tests/test_stack_mode_live_tool.py` tests `judge` offline, and `setup` / `teardown`
+against a stand-in `gh` and local git remotes. Nothing in CI runs the live steps.
